ished this  month again Larry Scahill here and some  of his colleagues have taken the lead in  this is that risperidone should not be  used alone and what they have done is  experimentally looking at this can they  complement can we complement a  medication such as risperidone with  psychosocial interventions in this case  with a structured parent training in  this case they used to manualized  intervention 11 sessions 12 sessions  there are a range of didactic materials  that included videos scripts specific  tasks that would be done with a child  reinforcement positive reinforcement for  doing things well some come from channel  communication skills helping the child  better identify needs and wants and  having behavioral rehearsal for problem  problematic behaviors with extensive  feedback and homework in doing this you  won't be surprised to find out that in  the combination group he represented in  in the blue line things went better than  in the risperidone only group the  difference is certainly not as dramatic  and in the past in the previous slide  but the point is that complementing  behavioral interventions to medication  gives the best of both worlds in this  case again we're comparing medication  alone to medication and psychosocial  intervention and seeing the added  benefits of adding  is intervention the FDA as you know is  involved in the marketing and the  branding of medications and the safety  of medications and this is a partial  list of the very few of the dozen or so  medications that have been approved by  the FDA for use in kids under the age of  18 this a partial list but you can see  that most of these medications have been  used for example methylphenidate or  Ritalin has been approved for the Ford  season attention deficit disorder  there's a range of antidepressants that  have been approved for use in  obsessive-compulsive disorder lithium  and mania some antipsychotics for  Tourette Syndrome haloperidol for  explosiveness imipramine fo