en and if those kids could  tolerate it then they went into a  four-week double-blind phase where they  were given either a placebo a sugar pill  or the the stimulant methylphenidate  ritalin and four different in three  different doses low a medium and a high  it was an eight-week study a four-week  double-blind study followed by an  eight-week extension and what is it that  was found well rather than just showing  you what was found let me compare that  to what we know about the treatment of  ADHD in kids without autism the MTA the  multi I'm blanking on the multi  something I'm blanking about what the  MTA it's one of these things that you  use how much multi-faceted multi  something treatment of ADHD this is the  largest study the the granddaddy of all  ADHD studies and I'm going to compare  what we know from that Rolls Royce study  in kids without autism to what we know  in autism so the first thing is that  most kids respond whether they have  autism or not but we see that the  response rate in the MTA and kids with  regular without autism was close to 80%  most kids respond and respond very  dramatically to  stimulants their inattention their  impulsivity gets better the first  observation is that in the case of  autism the response is not nearly as  much about 40% 50% of kids responded the  second observation is that this is not  accounted by placebo response because  the placebo response across these two  groups was essentially identical so the  kids responded just as poorly to placebo  they didn't respond to placebo when we  try to quantify that we see that  methylphenidate again riddling used  either in a low and a medium or a high  dose seems to have some efficacy for  autism  all of these are effect sizes in the  range of 0.3 to 0.5 compare that to what  we had talked about with autism that  with respect on those effect sizes of  1.2 that were very dramatic so these are  subtle these are small effect sizes  compared to what we know in normally  developi